<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=flying-buttress">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Building and engineering</p><h1 class="enc-title">Flying buttress</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1160s</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Arc-boutant, Arch buttress</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A flying buttress is a ramping arch that carries the outward thrust of a stone vault or roof from the upper part of a wall across open space to a massive pier standing away from it. Ancient examples survive on the Basilica of San Vitale in Ravenna and the Rotunda of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galerius">Galerius</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a>, and Romanesque churches such as Durham Cathedral hid similar arches under their gallery roofs. By the 1160s builders in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ile-de-france">Île-de-France</a> ran longer, visible arches over the aisles, as at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-germain-des-pres">Saint-Germain-des-Prés</a>, completed in 1163, and Notre Dame de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1180. Walls could then be thinner, with larger windows of stained glass, and the flying buttress became a defining feature of Gothic architecture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/flying-buttress.jpg" alt="Flying buttress" width="330" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photo: Andreas Praefcke · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lübeck_Marienkirche_Strebebögen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1160s</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Île-de-France (Saint-Germain-des-Prés, Paris)</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The flying buttress was first developed in late antiquity: early, undated examples stand on the Basilica of San Vitale in Ravenna and the Rotunda of Galerius in Thessaloniki.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/arch">Arch<span>before 3800 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=flying-buttress">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
